= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $c_s^2\sim P_g/\rho$. The assumed magnetic pressure gives
$$
\frac{P_m}{P_g}\sim\frac{\rho r\Omega c_s}{\rho c_s^2}
=\frac{r\Omega}{c_s}=\mathrm{Ma},
$$
so the <plasma beta> obeys $\boxed{\beta=P_g/P_m\sim\mathrm{Ma}^{-1}}$. In the magnetically dominated regime, vertical <hydrostatic equilibrium> gives $P_m/\rho\sim\Omega^2H^2$. Combining this with $P_m/\rho\sim r\Omega c_s$ yields
$$
\boxed{\frac Hr\sim\left(\frac{c_s}{r\Omega}\right)^{1/2}
\sim\mathrm{Ma}^{-1/2}}.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Order-of-magnitude vertical balance gives
$$
P\sim\rho\Omega^2H^2,\qquad
F\sim\alpha P\Omega H,\qquad
F\sim\frac{\sigma T^4}{\kappa\rho H},\qquad
\rho\sim\frac{\Sigma}{H}.
$$
Magnetic support also gives $\Omega^2H^2\sim r\Omega T^{1/2}$, so $T\sim\Omega^2H^4/r^2$. Eliminating $P,F,\rho,T$ gives
$$
H^{14}\propto\Sigma^2r^8\Omega^{-5}.
$$
For Keplerian $\Omega\propto r^{-3/2}$,
$$
\boxed{H\propto r^{31/28}\Sigma^{1/7}}.
$$
The <alpha disk> stress implies $\bar\nu\sim\alpha P/(\rho\Omega)\sim\alpha\Omega H^2$, and therefore
$$
\boxed{\bar\nu\propto r^{5/7}\Sigma^{2/7}},
\qquad
\boxed{a=\frac57,\quad b=\frac27}.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= 2
